1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:10,131
Two things land on your desk in the same
week. First: git status takes four seconds

2
00:00:10,171 --> 00:00:15,734
and git log on a path takes twenty. The
CTO asks, is the repository broken, or too

3
00:00:15,774 --> 00:00:21,268
big? Second: a consultant told the team
to put git gc --aggressive into a nightly

4
00:00:21,308 --> 00:00:25,162
cron job, a task the machine
runs on a timer. Should you?

5
00:00:25,202 --> 00:00:27,007
Yes or no: say it out loud.

6
00:00:31,847 --> 00:00:35,465
The answers from Chapter
26. To the first: neither.

7
00:00:35,505 --> 00:00:41,490
Slow commands map to specific kinds of size,
and each kind has a remedy that changes

8
00:00:41,530 --> 00:00:47,300
no history. To the second: no. Since Git
2.54, Git's automatic maintenance uses a

9
00:00:47,340 --> 00:00:53,325
strategy that avoids exactly that kind of
full rewrite, and git gc doesn't even take

10
00:00:53,365 --> 00:00:58,776
maintenance's lock. Hold on to both
answers. By the end you can defend them.

11
00:01:00,616 --> 00:01:04,330
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:01:04,370 --> 00:01:09,377
This video is about what Git does to its
own storage in the background, after your

13
00:01:09,417 --> 00:01:12,823
commit returns. That housekeeping
is called maintenance.

14
00:01:12,863 --> 00:01:17,932
You'll measure a repository along its
dimensions, trace one maintenance run command

15
00:01:17,972 --> 00:01:22,978
by command, watch packs form a geometric
progression over four runs, and see where

16
00:01:23,018 --> 00:01:28,087
unreachable objects go before they are
deleted. A pack is one file that stores many

17
00:01:28,127 --> 00:01:31,780
objects. An unreachable object
is one that no name leads to.

18
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:37,474
One principle first, in the textbook's
words: everything in this chapter is derived

19
00:01:37,514 --> 00:01:42,413
data or a way of leaving data out.
Derived data is data Git can rebuild from

20
00:01:42,453 --> 00:01:45,793
something else. No feature
here changes a commit ID.

21
00:01:45,833 --> 00:01:51,123
That's why these features are safe to adopt,
and why a slow repository is rarely a

22
00:01:51,163 --> 00:01:52,812
reason to rewrite history.

23
00:01:53,152 --> 00:01:59,882
The repository is orbit, the monorepo of
Chapter 24, one repository that holds many

24
00:01:59,922 --> 00:02:05,428
projects: 33 files, 94 commits on
main, a feature branch, five tags.

25
00:02:05,468 --> 00:02:07,794
Three replays from labs/ch26.

26
00:02:08,134 --> 00:02:11,682
One rule of this course: it never
runs git maintenance start.

27
00:02:11,722 --> 00:02:16,625
That command writes outside the sandbox. I
describe it from the manual and don't run

28
00:02:16,665 --> 00:02:16,801
it.

29
00:02:18,641 --> 00:02:20,053
After this video you can:

30
00:02:20,393 --> 00:02:24,986
Name the dimensions along which a repository
grows and the command that each slows.

31
00:02:25,326 --> 00:02:30,147
Say what runs when a commit triggers
automatic maintenance on Git 2.55.

32
00:02:30,487 --> 00:02:32,952
Trace one maintenance run task by task.

33
00:02:33,292 --> 00:02:36,811
Explain geometric repacking with
three packs of given sizes.

34
00:02:37,151 --> 00:02:41,685
Explain what a cruft pack holds and when
unreachable objects are finally deleted.

35
00:02:43,525 --> 00:02:48,533
Start with slowness. In one sentence: a Git
command is slow when the amount of data

36
00:02:48,573 --> 00:02:53,642
it must touch is large, so the diagnosis is
to find which data a command touches and

37
00:02:53,682 --> 00:02:56,318
then to make that amount
smaller or indexed.

38
00:02:56,658 --> 00:03:01,648
"Large" has dimensions that don't depend
on each other: tracked files, commits and

39
00:03:01,688 --> 00:03:06,617
trees, blob content in all versions, loose
objects and packs, and refs, the names

40
00:03:06,657 --> 00:03:10,604
such as branches and tags. Measure
them before you tune anything.

41
00:03:10,944 --> 00:03:15,838
git status, git add and git switch touch
every index entry and tracked file, and

42
00:03:15,878 --> 00:03:19,416
every directory. The index is
Git's list of tracked files.

43
00:03:19,456 --> 00:03:24,288
The remedies are sparse-checkout, the
sparse index, the untracked cache and the

44
00:03:24,328 --> 00:03:25,522
file-system monitor.

45
00:03:25,862 --> 00:03:30,722
git log on a path and git blame touch
every commit's tree, and the remedy is

46
00:03:30,762 --> 00:03:35,945
changed-path filters in the commit-graph.
git log --graph, git merge-base and git

47
00:03:35,985 --> 00:03:40,588
branch --contains touch commit objects
one by one, and the remedy is the

48
00:03:40,628 --> 00:03:41,426
commit-graph.

49
00:03:41,766 --> 00:03:46,463
Any object lookup touches one index per
pack. The remedies are repacking and the

50
00:03:46,503 --> 00:03:50,549
multi-pack-index. git clone and git
fetch touch everything reachable.

51
00:03:50,589 --> 00:03:53,984
The remedies are partial clone,
and bitmaps on the server.

52
00:03:54,024 --> 00:03:56,412
The next videos take those one at a time.

53
00:03:56,752 --> 00:04:02,090
Now maintenance. In one sentence: git gc
is one command that repacks everything and

54
00:04:02,130 --> 00:04:07,468
deletes what has expired, git maintenance
runs a configurable set of smaller tasks,

55
00:04:07,508 --> 00:04:12,911
and since Git 2.54 the maintenance that Git
starts on its own no longer runs git gc.

56
00:04:13,251 --> 00:04:17,215
git maintenance run, labelled
🟡 CAUTION, executes tasks.

57
00:04:17,255 --> 00:04:21,863
The manual lists them: gc,
commit-graph, prefetch, loose-objects,

58
00:04:21,903 --> 00:04:27,226
incremental-repack, pack-refs,
reflog-expire, rerere-gc and worktree-prune.

59
00:04:27,266 --> 00:04:32,803
Git 2.55 also accepts geometric-repack,
which the task list of its manual page

60
00:04:32,843 --> 00:04:33,232
omits.

61
00:04:33,572 --> 00:04:38,107
Which tasks run when you name none
is decided by maintenance.strategy.

62
00:04:38,147 --> 00:04:39,088
There are four.

63
00:04:39,428 --> 00:04:45,408
geometric runs commit-graph, geometric-repack,
pack-refs, reflog-expire, rerere-gc

64
00:04:45,448 --> 00:04:51,282
and worktree-prune. It deletes reflog
entries past their expiry, and unreachable

65
00:04:51,322 --> 00:04:57,229
objects only at an all-into-one repack.
It's the default for manual and automatic

66
00:04:57,269 --> 00:05:03,176
maintenance since Git 2.54. gc runs the
gc task, deletes what git gc deletes, and

67
00:05:03,216 --> 00:05:05,525
was the default before Git 2.54.

68
00:05:05,865 --> 00:05:09,823
incremental runs prefetch and
commit-graph hourly, loose-objects and

69
00:05:09,863 --> 00:05:14,232
incremental-repack daily, and pack-refs
weekly. It deletes no data, and git

70
00:05:14,272 --> 00:05:18,818
maintenance register sets it for scheduled
runs. none runs nothing and is what

71
00:05:18,858 --> 00:05:22,228
scheduled maintenance uses
until a strategy is configured.

72
00:05:22,568 --> 00:05:27,723
A caveat the textbook attaches: the manual
page of 2.55 is not consistent with

73
00:05:27,763 --> 00:05:33,185
itself. It calls geometric "the default
strategy for manual maintenance" and still

74
00:05:33,225 --> 00:05:38,115
says in two places that "by default,
only maintenance.gc.enabled is true".

75
00:05:38,155 --> 00:05:40,313
The trace in the demo settles it.

76
00:05:40,653 --> 00:05:45,994
Maintenance runs in three ways. Automatic:
commands that write objects finish by

77
00:05:46,034 --> 00:05:51,173
starting git maintenance run --auto,
detached, and each task runs only if its

78
00:05:51,213 --> 00:05:56,487
threshold is met. Manual: you run it.
Scheduled: git maintenance start, also 🟡,

79
00:05:56,527 --> 00:06:01,733
registers the repository in your global
configuration, installs entries in the

80
00:06:01,773 --> 00:06:06,778
system scheduler, launchd on macOS,
sets the incremental strategy, and sets

81
00:06:06,818 --> 00:06:09,535
maintenance.auto=false in the repository.

82
00:06:09,875 --> 00:06:15,090
Geometric repacking, in one sentence: it
keeps packs in a sequence where each is at

83
00:06:15,130 --> 00:06:20,217
least twice as large as the next, by merging
only the small ones, so that regular

84
00:06:20,257 --> 00:06:24,649
maintenance never rewrites the large
pack. Size is counted in objects.

85
00:06:24,689 --> 00:06:29,587
The cost of a run is then proportional
to the new data, not to the repository.

86
00:06:29,627 --> 00:06:34,714
git gc and git repack -a rewrite everything
every time, and on a large repository

87
00:06:34,754 --> 00:06:38,386
that difference decides whether
maintenance can run daily.

88
00:06:38,726 --> 00:06:43,711
Cruft packs, in one sentence: a cruft pack
is a pack that holds only unreachable

89
00:06:43,751 --> 00:06:48,988
objects, with a companion file recording
when each was last touched, so that garbage

90
00:06:49,028 --> 00:06:53,511
can wait out its grace period without
becoming thousands of loose files.

91
00:06:53,551 --> 00:06:58,410
The grace period is gc.pruneExpire, two
weeks, and it exists because a running

92
00:06:58,450 --> 00:07:01,425
command may be about to
reference those objects.

93
00:07:01,765 --> 00:07:06,413
The companion is a .mtimes file. Under
the geometric strategy a cruft pack is

94
00:07:06,453 --> 00:07:11,527
written only when the repack is all-into-one.
An ordinary geometric run doesn't look

95
00:07:11,567 --> 00:07:12,927
at reachability at all.

96
00:07:13,267 --> 00:07:17,965
When should you not interfere? Leave
automatic maintenance on for ordinary

97
00:07:18,005 --> 00:07:21,551
repositories. And don't put
git gc into cron next to it.

98
00:07:21,591 --> 00:07:26,865
The manual says: "git gc modifies the object
database but does not take the lock in

99
00:07:26,905 --> 00:07:32,180
the same way as git maintenance run. If
possible, use git maintenance run --task=gc

100
00:07:32,220 --> 00:07:37,046
instead of git gc." As for --aggressive:
it discards all existing deltas and

101
00:07:37,086 --> 00:07:42,360
recomputes them, and the manual's own verdict
is that it is "probably not worth it"

102
00:07:42,400 --> 00:07:44,858
without benchmarks for your repository.

103
00:07:46,698 --> 00:07:50,990
The textbook's analogy: git gc closes
the warehouse for a full inventory and

104
00:07:51,030 --> 00:07:55,494
reshelving. git maintenance sends a clerk
through one aisle at a time while the

105
00:07:55,534 --> 00:07:56,691
warehouse stays open.

106
00:07:57,031 --> 00:08:02,085
The analogy breaks at deletion. A full
inventory is also the moment when unclaimed

107
00:08:02,125 --> 00:08:06,248
goods are thrown away, and the
clerk's rounds postpone that moment.

108
00:08:06,288 --> 00:08:11,404
Under the default strategy, unreachable
objects can sit inside the large pack for a

109
00:08:11,444 --> 00:08:14,261
long time, because nothing
rewrites that pack.

110
00:08:16,101 --> 00:08:22,775
The first run put all 507 objects into one
pack. At run 2, the 24 new loose objects

111
00:08:22,815 --> 00:08:29,570
become a pack of their own. At run 3, Git
looks at the packs that exist, 507 and 24.

112
00:08:29,610 --> 00:08:36,123
They are in progression, 507 is at least
twice 24, so nothing existing is merged,

113
00:08:36,163 --> 00:08:41,947
and the new loose objects again become
a pack of their own: 507, 24, 24.

114
00:08:42,287 --> 00:08:48,356
Try it now, on paper, thirty seconds.
Before run 4 the packs are 507, 24 and 24.

115
00:08:48,396 --> 00:08:53,396
Check the rule pair by pair: is
each pack at least twice the next?

116
00:08:53,436 --> 00:08:56,297
Which packs will Git merge? I'll wait.

117
00:09:01,137 --> 00:09:07,829
At run 4 the existing packs are 507, 24
and 24. The two small ones break the rule,

118
00:09:07,869 --> 00:09:14,724
because 24 isn't at least twice 24. They
merge with the new objects into one pack of

119
00:09:14,764 --> 00:09:21,292
104. And 507 is at least twice 104. Notice
the caption: the large pack was never

120
00:09:21,332 --> 00:09:22,112
rewritten.

121
00:09:26,452 --> 00:09:31,273
Four dimensions in five commands:
history, working tree, refs, storage.

122
00:09:31,313 --> 00:09:35,176
Look at the last three lines:
507 loose objects, no pack.

123
00:09:35,216 --> 00:09:38,051
This repository has never been maintained.

124
00:09:38,391 --> 00:09:44,506
You saw this pipeline in video 100. One
43 KB file with 13 versions is where the

125
00:09:44,546 --> 00:09:45,967
bytes of orbit are.

126
00:09:46,307 --> 00:09:49,707
To see where one command
spends its effort, ask Git.

127
00:09:49,747 --> 00:09:54,007
GIT_TRACE2_PERF=1 makes any command
print a table of what it did.

128
00:09:54,047 --> 00:09:58,506
The rows of type data carry counters,
and counters are reproducible.

129
00:09:58,846 --> 00:10:04,648
33 tracked files. How many lstat calls,
questions to the filesystem about one file,

130
00:10:04,688 --> 00:10:08,238
does one git status make?
Say your number out loud.

131
00:10:13,078 --> 00:10:18,533
Thirty-three index entries read, 33 lstat
calls, 20 directories and 53 paths visited

132
00:10:18,573 --> 00:10:23,898
to look for untracked files. Scale those
numbers to 500,000 files and you have the

133
00:10:23,938 --> 00:10:27,824
four seconds of the hook. That's
the first answer, measured.

134
00:10:30,664 --> 00:10:36,922
A quick quiz. A commit in a brand-new
repository. What does Git start as its last

135
00:10:36,962 --> 00:10:42,054
act: git gc --auto, git maintenance
run --auto, or nothing at all?

136
00:10:42,094 --> 00:10:42,987
Your answer?

137
00:10:47,827 --> 00:10:52,928
git maintenance run --auto --quiet
--detach. Not git gc --auto.

138
00:10:52,968 --> 00:10:59,619
Then, in orbit, the demo switches the
trigger off, so that nothing runs behind our

139
00:10:59,659 --> 00:11:00,027
back.

140
00:11:00,367 --> 00:11:06,168
git maintenance is-needed needs Git 2.53
or later and answers with its exit status:

141
00:11:06,208 --> 00:11:12,009
0 means yes. The repack is needed, because
more than about 100 loose objects exist.

142
00:11:12,049 --> 00:11:16,794
The commit-graph is not: fewer than
100 commits are missing from it.

143
00:11:16,834 --> 00:11:21,439
And the old gc threshold of about
6,700 loose objects is far away.

144
00:11:21,479 --> 00:11:26,858
The loose count is an estimate: Git
counts the files in one of the 256 object

145
00:11:26,898 --> 00:11:28,758
directories and multiplies.

146
00:11:29,098 --> 00:11:33,099
Now the 🟡 command, in the foreground,
with the trace written to a file.

147
00:11:33,439 --> 00:11:37,357
How many commands will maintenance
start, and is gc one of them?

148
00:11:37,397 --> 00:11:38,346
Say it out loud.

149
00:11:43,186 --> 00:11:48,298
Six commands, and no git gc among them:
pack-refs, reflog expire, repack,

150
00:11:48,338 --> 00:11:54,155
commit-graph write, worktree prune, rerere
gc. The repack line says --cruft with no

151
00:11:54,195 --> 00:11:59,448
--geometric, because there was no pack
yet and so everything went into one.

152
00:11:59,488 --> 00:12:05,306
Below, repack started pack-objects twice,
once for reachable objects and once for a

153
00:12:05,346 --> 00:12:10,105
cruft pack that turned out empty,
and then wrote a multi-pack-index.

154
00:12:16,445 --> 00:12:21,569
One pack with its index and reverse index,
a multi-pack-index, a commit-graph stored

155
00:12:21,609 --> 00:12:25,688
as a chain, and every ref moved
from its own file into packed-refs.

156
00:12:25,728 --> 00:12:29,684
No loose ref files remain. Remember
that line for the next video.

157
00:12:30,024 --> 00:12:34,901
The state table for git maintenance run.
Working tree, index and HEAD are unchanged.

158
00:12:34,941 --> 00:12:38,530
The current branch ref has the
same value, now in packed-refs.

159
00:12:38,570 --> 00:12:43,038
Loose objects are packed, the multi-pack-index
and the commit-graph chain are

160
00:12:43,078 --> 00:12:46,081
written, and reflog entries
past expiry are removed.

161
00:12:46,121 --> 00:12:48,189
The remote and GitHub are unchanged.

162
00:12:48,529 --> 00:12:50,628
Six commits later, the second run.

163
00:12:50,968 --> 00:12:56,226
Now the repack line reads
--geometric=2. Two packs afterwards.

164
00:12:56,566 --> 00:13:04,601
This is the diagram, measured. First
507 and 24. Then 507, 24 and 24.

165
00:13:04,641 --> 00:13:06,590
Then 507 and 104.

166
00:13:06,930 --> 00:13:08,526
For comparison, the older way.

167
00:13:08,866 --> 00:13:14,171
Here git gc appears, with git prune, which
deletes loose unreachable objects past

168
00:13:14,211 --> 00:13:19,715
the grace period. It leaves one pack of all
611 objects, a single commit-graph file,

169
00:13:19,755 --> 00:13:21,298
and no multi-pack-index.

170
00:13:21,638 --> 00:13:24,834
The tasks of the incremental
strategy, run once by name.

171
00:13:24,874 --> 00:13:28,300
None of these deletes an object
that isn't stored elsewhere.

172
00:13:28,640 --> 00:13:33,822
This replay contains two 🔴 commands,
so the five answers first.

173
00:13:33,862 --> 00:13:39,127
git reflog expire --expire=now
--all deletes every reflog entry.

174
00:13:39,167 --> 00:13:45,676
It destroys the record of where refs used
to point, the safety net of recovery.

175
00:13:45,716 --> 00:13:50,898
Preview it with git reflog expire
--dry-run --expire=now --all.

176
00:13:50,938 --> 00:13:53,882
There's no recovery for the entries.

177
00:13:54,222 --> 00:13:58,000
git fsck still finds the
objects until they're pruned.

178
00:13:58,040 --> 00:14:01,888
git gc --prune=now deletes
unreachable objects at once.

179
00:14:01,928 --> 00:14:07,474
Preview with git fsck --unreachable, and
recover from another clone or a backup

180
00:14:07,514 --> 00:14:12,989
only. Both are appropriate after a
deliberate history rewrite, in a repository

181
00:14:13,029 --> 00:14:18,574
you've copied first, never as routine
maintenance. Here they run in a throwaway

182
00:14:18,614 --> 00:14:19,140
sandbox.

183
00:14:19,480 --> 00:14:24,635
Watch two commits in this replay: 9c9fd21
and 88222b7, the work of one feature

184
00:14:24,675 --> 00:14:30,030
branch. Once the branch and the reflog
entries are gone, they're unreachable, and

185
00:14:30,070 --> 00:14:32,761
now the question is when they're deleted.

186
00:14:36,101 --> 00:14:41,069
After the branch is deleted, nothing is
unreachable: the reflog of HEAD still names

187
00:14:41,109 --> 00:14:45,413
the commits. After the reflogs are
expired, ten objects are unreachable.

188
00:14:45,753 --> 00:14:49,326
A quick quiz. A maintenance
run of the default strategy.

189
00:14:49,366 --> 00:14:54,422
How many of the ten unreachable objects
does it delete: all ten, some, or none?

190
00:14:54,462 --> 00:14:55,196
Your answer?

191
00:15:00,036 --> 00:15:05,032
None. It packed three new objects and left
the ten where they were: inside the large

192
00:15:05,072 --> 00:15:09,949
pack, which a geometric run doesn't rewrite.
If you said all ten, that's what most

193
00:15:09,989 --> 00:15:11,748
people expect from a clean-up.

194
00:15:12,088 --> 00:15:18,086
The gc task separates them. The pack that
has an .mtimes file is the cruft pack: ten

195
00:15:18,126 --> 00:15:23,046
objects. The deleted branch's commit
88222b7 is still readable by ID.

196
00:15:23,386 --> 00:15:27,150
With no grace period, the cruft
pack is gone and so is the commit.

197
00:15:27,190 --> 00:15:31,761
The transcript uses now because a cut-off
that depends on the clock could not be

198
00:15:31,801 --> 00:15:32,396
reproduced.

199
00:15:34,236 --> 00:15:35,884
Five mistakes to watch for.

200
00:15:36,224 --> 00:15:41,197
Scheduling git gc next to automatic
maintenance. Root cause: git gc modifies the

201
00:15:41,237 --> 00:15:45,647
object database without taking the
lock that git maintenance run takes.

202
00:15:45,987 --> 00:15:50,809
Treating several packs as a fault. Root
cause: under geometric repacking, several

203
00:15:50,849 --> 00:15:53,871
packs are the normal state
of a healthy repository.

204
00:15:54,211 --> 00:15:57,443
Assuming a removed commit is
deleted after maintenance.

205
00:15:57,483 --> 00:16:02,204
Root cause: an ordinary geometric run does
not look at reachability; unreachable

206
00:16:02,244 --> 00:16:07,143
objects leave only at an all-into-one repack,
and then wait in a cruft pack for the

207
00:16:07,183 --> 00:16:07,916
grace period.

208
00:16:08,256 --> 00:16:14,419
Looking for garbage as loose files. Root
cause: unreachable objects sit in packs;

209
00:16:14,459 --> 00:16:20,392
diagnose with git fsck --unreachable |
wc -l and by looking for .mtimes files.

210
00:16:20,732 --> 00:16:25,311
Tuning before measuring. Root cause: the
dimensions of size are independent, and

211
00:16:25,351 --> 00:16:27,043
each slows different commands.

212
00:16:28,883 --> 00:16:33,996
Now, out of the lab. Suppose a team removed
a leaked credential from the history of

213
00:16:34,036 --> 00:16:37,906
its evaluation repository, rewrote
the branch and force-pushed.

214
00:16:37,946 --> 00:16:42,935
A week later a security review finds the
old blob still readable in a developer's

215
00:16:42,975 --> 00:16:47,901
clone. Nothing is broken. That clone runs
the default strategy, and a repository

216
00:16:47,941 --> 00:16:52,370
under the default strategy can hold
unreachable objects for a long time.

217
00:16:52,410 --> 00:16:56,592
Rewriting and force-pushing doesn't
delete the objects from a clone.

218
00:16:56,632 --> 00:16:59,882
The purge is the deliberate
procedure of Chapter 21B.

219
00:17:00,222 --> 00:17:05,031
For the nightly cron question, the same
team's decision: leave automatic maintenance

220
00:17:05,071 --> 00:17:09,650
on for ordinary repositories. For the very
large repository, use git maintenance

221
00:17:09,690 --> 00:17:14,384
start on developer machines, which is what
scalar does, and know what it installs.

222
00:17:16,224 --> 00:17:20,456
Your turn. Do Lab 16.3, "Trace
what a maintenance run does", in

223
00:17:20,496 --> 00:17:26,083
lab-manual/m16-maintenance.md. Before you
read the trace, write down which commands

224
00:17:26,123 --> 00:17:30,964
you expect maintenance to start, in
order, and whether gc is among them.

225
00:17:31,004 --> 00:17:35,168
Before each later run, predict
the object count of every pack.

226
00:17:35,508 --> 00:17:41,479
The challenge is Exercise 16.6,
"The largest blobs in history", in

227
00:17:41,519 --> 00:17:44,302
exercises/m16-m18-internals.md.

228
00:17:46,142 --> 00:17:48,672
Question 82 of the CTO question bank:

229
00:17:49,012 --> 00:17:53,652
A strong answer names the trigger and the
strategy, lists the tasks rather than

230
00:17:53,692 --> 00:17:57,681
saying "it cleans up", and contrasts
what is rewritten in each case.

231
00:17:57,721 --> 00:18:02,598
On deletion it's exact about three things:
reflog entries, unreachable objects, and

232
00:18:02,638 --> 00:18:06,805
the grace period. It mentions the
version in which the default changed.

233
00:18:08,645 --> 00:18:12,934
Let's land this. Measure before you
tune, let maintenance do its rounds, and

234
00:18:12,974 --> 00:18:15,497
remember that unreachable
is not yet deleted.

235
00:18:15,837 --> 00:18:17,497
You should now be able to say:

236
00:18:17,837 --> 00:18:22,653
A command is slow because of the data it
touches; measure files, commits, blobs,

237
00:18:22,693 --> 00:18:24,232
packs and refs separately.

238
00:18:24,572 --> 00:18:30,390
Since Git 2.54, a commit triggers git
maintenance run --auto with the geometric

239
00:18:30,430 --> 00:18:33,283
strategy, and that does not run git gc.

240
00:18:33,623 --> 00:18:38,734
Geometric repacking merges only small packs,
so the large pack is never rewritten.

241
00:18:39,074 --> 00:18:43,957
Unreachable objects are separated into a
cruft pack only at an all-into-one repack,

242
00:18:43,997 --> 00:18:46,270
and are deleted after the grace period.

243
00:18:46,610 --> 00:18:48,454
Nothing here changes a commit ID.

244
00:18:50,294 --> 00:18:55,196
Read sections 26.1 to 26.5 of Chapter 26.

245
00:18:55,536 --> 00:19:00,495
Today you traced what Git does after a commit
returns, and you answered a consultant

246
00:19:00,535 --> 00:19:05,494
with a measurement. Do the lab before the
next video: refs in depth, from loose refs

247
00:19:05,534 --> 00:19:09,719
to reflog storage. Until then, look
at the state first and type second.

248
00:19:09,759 --> 00:19:11,148
See you in the next one.
